Third-party software in Maktaba's server image
==============================================

The server image (ghcr.io/hamza-labs-core/maktaba-server, deploy/docker/server/Dockerfile)
installs these Debian 12 packages beside Maktaba itself. Each keeps its own licence; the
full texts are in the image at /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright.

VA-API video drivers (amd64 only; Epic 59 story 59.05)
-------------------------------------------------------

intel-media-va-driver-non-free (Debian 12, component non-free)
  Intel Media Driver for VAAPI (iHD): Intel GPUs from Broadwell on (the J4125, N100, N5105).
  Source: https://github.com/intel/media-driver
  Licence: Expat (MIT); its test framework BSD-3-Clause. Copyright 2007-2021 Intel
  Corporation and others.
  Why non-free: Debian builds it with Intel's prebuilt GPU kernels, which come without
  source and cannot be rebuilt; their licence is the same Expat licence, which permits
  redistribution, so the image may carry the package as Debian ships it.

i965-va-driver (Debian 12, main)
  The VA-API driver for older Intel GPUs (i965). Licence: Expat (MIT).

mesa-va-drivers (Debian 12, main)
  Mesa's VA-API drivers (radeonsi for AMD GPUs and APUs). Licence: Expat (MIT) and others
  as listed in its copyright file.

vainfo (Debian 12, main)
  Lists a VA-API device's profiles; the streaming role reads them. Licence: Expat (MIT).

Other packages
--------------

ffmpeg (LGPL-2.1+ / GPL-2+ as Debian builds it), tini (MIT), libnss-wrapper (BSD-3-Clause),
ca-certificates (MPL-2.0 / GPL-2+), tzdata (public domain), avahi-utils and the Avahi
client libraries it brings (LGPL-2.1+; avahi-publish-service, run only when the host's system
D-Bus socket is mounted).

Third-party software in the Maktaba Player for Windows and Linux
================================================================

The Player (apps/desktop-player) carries libmpv, built by apps/desktop-player/libmpv/build.sh
from the sources pinned in apps/desktop-player/libmpv/pins.env (Epic 13 story 13.18), in its
install folder (lib/app/resources on Linux, app\resources on Windows), and loads it
dynamically. The build fails if FFmpeg or mpv is configured GPL or non-free. Each part keeps
its own licence; the texts and the exact configure lines are beside the library, in
licences/ and BUILD-INFO.txt.

mpv 0.42.0, built as libmpv with -Dgpl=false
  https://mpv.io, source https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv. Licence: LGPL-2.1-or-later.

FFmpeg 8.1.3, built without --enable-gpl and --enable-nonfree, inside libmpv
  https://ffmpeg.org, source https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-8.1.3.tar.xz.
  Licence: LGPL-2.1-or-later.

libplacebo 7.387.0, inside libmpv
  Source https://code.videolan.org/videolan/libplacebo (mirror github.com/haasn/libplacebo).
  Licence: LGPL-2.1-or-later.

dav1d 1.5.5 (AV1 decoding), inside libmpv
  Source https://code.videolan.org/videolan/dav1d. Licence: BSD-2-Clause.

On Linux libmpv uses the system's libass (ISC), FreeType (FTL), FriBidi (LGPL-2.1-or-later),
HarfBuzz (MIT), ALSA's and PulseAudio's client libraries (LGPL-2.1-or-later) and libva (MIT),
which the .deb and .rpm depend on. On Windows the same libraries come from MSYS2's UCRT64
packages, beside libmpv-2.dll; BUILD-INFO.txt lists each DLL with its package and licence.

The Player also carries the JetBrains Runtime 25 (GPL-2.0 with the Classpath Exception,
https://github.com/JetBrains/JetBrainsRuntime) and, for one more release, OpenJFX
(GPL-2.0 with the Classpath Exception).
